"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8075],{38075:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-04","slug":"base-04","trackId":"base","order":5,"title":"Networks, Tokens and Gas","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":12,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-04"],"markdown":"Lesson 4 \xb7 8 min\\n\\n## Networks, Tokens and Gas\\n\\nHow to Use a Wallet\\n\\nYou now have a wallet. Next, you need to understand what it displays and why transfers involve networks, tokens and fees. It initially looks more complicated than it is. Let us take it step by step.\\n\\n### A Network Is a Road\\n\\nImagine each blockchain as a separate transport system. Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) is one road. Solana ↗ (opens in a new tab) is another. Bitcoin is a third.\\n\\nArbitrum, Base, BNB Chain and others are separate networks too. Each has its own rules, speed and transfer cost. Sending cryptocurrency always happens on a particular network. That is why a wallet or exchange asks before a transfer:\\n\\nWhich network do you want to use?\\n\\nThis is one of the most important questions, because the same asset can exist on several networks. USDT, for example, exists on Ethereum, Tron, Solana and other networks. The name is always USDT.\\n\\nTechnically, however, these are different versions of USDT on different blockchains. Think of it this way:\\n\\nUSDT on Ethereum is a car on one road. USDT on Solana is the same kind of car on a different road. It cannot simply jump from one road to the other.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### A Token Is What Moves Through the Network\\n\\nThink of the network as a road and a token as the cargo traveling along it. USDT and USDC are tokens that can exist on different networks. Many networks also have a native coin. For example:\\n- Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) is the network; ETH is its native coin.\\n- Solana ↗ (opens in a new tab) is the network; SOL is its native coin.\\n- Bitcoin is the network; BTC is its native coin.\\n\\nUSDT is a token that can exist on several networks. Keep the distinction clear:\\n\\nA network is the infrastructure processing transactions.\\n\\nA coin or token is an asset you own and can transfer.\\n\\nThis is one of the most common sources of confusion for beginners.\\n\\n### A Wallet Address Is Like an Account Number\\n\\nTo send cryptocurrency to someone, you need their address. An address may look like this:\\n\\n0x7A...91F3\\n\\nOr it may look completely different, depending on the network. Think of it as a bank account number. When sending cryptocurrency, you specify:\\n- the recipient;\\n- the amount;\\n- the network that should process the transfer.\\n\\nAfter confirmation, the network processes the transaction. There is an important difference from banking:\\n\\nif you send cryptocurrency to the wrong place, the transfer usually cannot be reversed.\\n\\nAlways verify the address before sending. Check that it matches the recipient’s address completely. Comparing only the first and last characters does not always prevent address substitution. For a large amount, send a small test transfer first.\\n\\n### One Address Can Work on Multiple Networks\\n\\nAnother feature can initially be confusing. When you open a wallet, you see its long sequence of letters and numbers. For example:\\n\\n0x71C7656EC7ab88b098defB751B7401B5f6d8976F\\n\\nSome addresses begin with 0x, followed by letters and numbers.\\n\\nThis format is used by several networks, including Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab), Base, Arbitrum and BNB Chain. You do not need to memorize every name; understand the principle. One wallet can have exactly the same address on all these networks.\\n\\nThat does not mean the funds are in the same place. Imagine having accounts at two different banks whose account numbers happen to match.\\n\\nThe number is identical, but the banks and balances are separate.\\n\\nCryptocurrency is similar. You can receive USDT at the same address on Ethereum and Arbitrum, but those are two separate balances on different networks. Checking the address alone is therefore insufficient.\\n\\nAlways check both the address and the network used for the transfer.\\n\\n### Why You Must Select a Network in the Wallet\\n\\nSuppose you want to send a friend 100 USDT held on Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab). Your friend’s wallet must support receiving USDT specifically on Ethereum. If the address was provided for another network and you send without checking compatibility, problems may arise.\\n\\nBefore every transfer, check:\\n- Which asset am I sending?\\n- Which network holds it?\\n- Does the recipient support this asset on the chosen network?\\n\\nRemember a simple rule:\\n\\nboth sides of the transfer must support the asset and network.\\n\\nThis matters particularly when moving funds between an exchange and a wallet. If the exchange offers several networks, do not automatically choose the cheapest. First confirm the destination supports the required network.\\n\\n### What Is Gas?\\n\\nGas is the fee for executing operations on certain blockchains. In everyday speech, people often use it to mean the network fee. When you send cryptocurrency, someone must process and confirm the transaction. The network charges for that work.\\n\\nThink of gas as a road toll. To perform an operation, you pay for its execution. Fees vary by network. One transfer may cost a few cents.\\n\\nAnother may cost several dollars. During high network load, it can cost even more.\\n\\n#### Why the Fee Is Not Paid in the Token You Are Sending\\n\\nSuppose you hold 1 000 USDT on Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) and want to send 100 USDT to a friend. The wallet says:\\n\\nInsufficient ETH for the fee.\\n\\nWhy? USDT is a token, whereas Ethereum network fees are generally paid in its native coin, ETH. You therefore need some ETH to send USDT on Ethereum. Other networks follow a similar principle:\\n- Ethereum — fees in ETH.\\n- Solana ↗ (opens in a new tab) — fees in SOL.\\n- BNB Chain — fees in BNB.\\n- Tron — operations consume network resources; if these are insufficient, TRX is used.\\n\\nImagine transporting a box of USDT along the Ethereum road, whose toll booths accept only ETH. What is inside the vehicle does not matter; the toll is paid in the local currency.\\n\\nKeep some of the native coin for the network you use. Otherwise, you can encounter an awkward situation:\\n\\nyour wallet contains thousands of dollars in tokens, but you cannot transfer them because you lack a few dollars for the fee.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### What Happens When You Press Send\\n\\nPut it together. To send a friend 100 USDT, you:\\n- Open the wallet.\\n- Select the network and USDT.\\n- Press Send.\\n- Paste the recipient’s address.\\n- Enter the transfer amount.\\n- Proceed to transaction confirmation.\\n\\nBefore confirming, carefully check:\\n- Network — the transfer network.\\n- Amount — the transfer amount.\\n- Network Fee — the fee charged.\\n- From — the sending address.\\n- To — the receiving address.\\n\\nIf everything is correct, confirm. The transaction is submitted to the network, checked and added to the blockchain. After some time, the recipient sees the funds.\\n\\nMany technical processes happen underneath. As a user, you only need to understand this sequence.\\n\\nYou are sending a friend 100 USDT. Is everything ready?\\n\\nYour USDT is on a network.\\n\\nYour friend expects a network. They differ: your USDT is on Solana, while your friend expects Ethereum. The transfer could be lost and cannot be reversed.\\n\\n### Why Funds Sometimes “Do Not Appear”\\n\\nSomeone receives cryptocurrency, opens the wallet and thinks:\\n\\nThe money has disappeared.\\n\\nThe issue may be much simpler. The wallet may not display the token automatically. The cryptocurrency is already at your address, but the app has not shown it on screen. Understand the distinction:\\n- Blockchain — the record of how much cryptocurrency is at your address.\\n- Wallet — the application through which you view and control those funds.\\n\\nA token missing from the wallet display does not automatically mean the money is gone.\\n\\n### How to Check a Wallet Through DeBank\\n\\nSpecial services let you check which cryptocurrency is at an address even if the wallet does not display it. One popular service is DeBank ↗ (opens in a new tab). Official website: https://debank.com ↗ (opens in a new tab). Enter a public wallet address in DeBank to view:\\n- the tokens at the address;\\n- the supported networks holding them;\\n- current asset values;\\n- transaction history;\\n- DeFi positions;\\n- an overview across several networks.\\n\\nThis is especially useful if you use several blockchains and do not want to keep switching networks in the wallet. DeBank does not support every network, however. It is primarily useful for Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab) and compatible blockchains.\\n\\nImportant: viewing a wallet in DeBank does not require a seed phrase or private key.\\n\\nThe public address is sufficient. Like a bank account number, you can share it so another person can send you funds. The seed phrase and private key, in contrast, grant access to the money itself and must never be shared. If funds seem to have “disappeared,” check the address in DeBank and see whether the asset appears on the expected network.\\n\\nA token missing from DeBank does not prove it is lost either. The service may not support that network or asset. For a definitive transaction check, use the relevant blockchain’s transaction explorer. Treat DeBank as an observation tool for your funds.\\n\\nA wallet manages cryptocurrency. DeBank provides a quick overview.\\n\\n### The Essentials\\n\\nIf you forget every term, remember three comparisons:\\n\\nA network is a road.\\n\\nA token is what travels along the road.\\n\\nGas is the toll.\\n\\nAnd a few simple rules:\\n- Always verify the network before a transfer.\\n- Confirm the recipient supports the asset on that network.\\n- Keep some native coin to pay fees.\\n- Before a large transfer, send a test transaction.\\n- If a token does not appear in the wallet, check the address through DeBank ↗ (opens in a new tab) or a blockchain explorer.\\n\\nMany beginner mistakes come from confusing tokens, networks and fees. Once this logic is clear, using a crypto wallet becomes much easier. The next article examines one of cryptocurrency’s most popular tools: stablecoins USDT, USDC and DAI.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nYou hold USDT, and the exchange offers withdrawals over Ethereum, Tron or Solana. What should you do first?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **DAI** — A stablecoin backed not by dollars but by an excess of other crypto collateral in a smart contract. A more “honest” decentralized option, but also a more complex one\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Seed Phrase** — 12 or 24 words used to restore a wallet. Whoever has the phrase controls the money, so neither support staff nor project administrators need to ask for it\\n- **Private Key** — What allows you to control the cryptocurrency at an address. The crypto itself is recorded on the blockchain; the wallet stores the keys, which are what you need to protect\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: You hold USDT, and the exchange offers withdrawals over Ethereum, Tron or Solana. What should you do first?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Choose the cheapest network\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Check which network the recipient’s address supports\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Always choose Ethereum\\n\\n**Question 2: You hold 1 000 USDT on Ethereum, but the wallet will not send it because you lack ETH. Why?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees\\n- ⚪ Option 2: USDT can only be sent with an equal amount of ETH\\n- ⚪ Option 3: USDT is temporarily frozen\\n\\n**Question 3: What should you do if a token does not appear in your wallet app?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Immediately assume the funds are lost\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Create a new wallet\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 3: Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-04-1","prompt":"You hold USDT, and the exchange offers withdrawals over Ethereum, Tron or Solana. What should you do first?","options":[{"id":"base-04-1-1","label":"Choose the cheapest network","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check which network the recipient’s address supports"},{"id":"base-04-1-2","label":"Check which network the recipient’s address supports","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Check which network the recipient’s address supports"},{"id":"base-04-1-3","label":"Always choose Ethereum","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check which network the recipient’s address supports"}],"explanation":"Check which network the recipient’s address supports","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:840–843 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-04-2","prompt":"You hold 1 000 USDT on Ethereum, but the wallet will not send it because you lack ETH. Why?","options":[{"id":"base-04-2-1","label":"ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees"},{"id":"base-04-2-2","label":"USDT can only be sent with an equal amount of ETH","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees"},{"id":"base-04-2-3","label":"USDT is temporarily frozen","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees"}],"explanation":"ETH is needed to pay Ethereum network fees","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:845–848 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-04-3","prompt":"What should you do if a token does not appear in your wallet app?","options":[{"id":"base-04-3-1","label":"Immediately assume the funds are lost","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer"},{"id":"base-04-3-2","label":"Create a new wallet","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer"},{"id":"base-04-3-3","label":"Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer"}],"explanation":"Check the address and required network through DeBank or a blockchain explorer","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:850–853 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":621,"lineEnd":858}}')}}]);